<?xml version="1.0" encoding="ISO-8859-1"?>
<faqs title="Frequently Asked Questions">

  <part id="general">
    <title>General</title>
   
    <faq id="reference">
      <question>
       Where do I find more documentations and references?
      </question>
      <answer>
       The <a href="apidocs/index.html">Javadoc</a> is a great source of documentations.
       This API relies heavily on generated code from this <a href="rss20.xsd">schema</a>. If you understand XML schmea, then you
       should take a look at the schema, it will give you some insights on how objects are mapped to xml and 
       vice versa.
      </answer>
    </faq>
    
    <faq id="more-info">
      <question>
       Where do I get more supports?
      </question>
      <answer>
       Use <a href="http://sourceforge.net/forum/?group_id=203266">support forum</a>
       and <a href="http://sourceforge.net/mail/?group_id=203266">user mailing list</a>.
       (Mailing list is the preferred way).
      </answer>
    </faq>

  </part>
  <part id="FeedReader">
    <title>FeedReader</title>
   
    <faq id="conditionalGet">
      <question>
       The FeedReader does not support Http request header, how do I do conditional get?
      </question>
      <answer>
       The current implementation of FeedReader does not add any additional request header
       when it issues a read request. If you need to perform conditional get (see
       <a href="http://fishbowl.pastiche.org/2002/10/21/http_conditional_get_for_rss_hackers">here</a>
       for details), you can:
       <br/>
		1. use the static read channel method <br/>
		2. extend the FeedReader class and override the method: <br/>  
		protected InputStream getStream() throws IOException{}  
		<br/>
		perhaps throw a special runtime exception when you decide not to read the stream.  
		 <br/>
		3. write a wrapper class to wrap the FeedReader class and manage the http stream in that class and pass it to FeedReader. 
		<br/>
		(also see <a href="http://sourceforge.net/forum/forum.php?thread_id=1823062&amp;forum_id=725344">this discussion thread</a>).
      </answer>
    </faq>
  </part>  
</faqs>